<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1920 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Red Army Is the Strongest</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Красная Армия всех сильней</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Krasnaya Armiya vsekh silney</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Red Army marching song from Russia's civil war in 1920, later sung with new words by workers' movements in Austria and Germany.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Pavel Gorinshtein</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Samuel Pokrass</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Red Army in the Russian Civil War; later communist movements in Europe</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Soviet Russia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1920; first printed 1925</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Pavel Gorinshtein, music by Samuel Pokrass (1920; first printed 1925))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest</a>&quot;, widely known by its opening words as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Army</a>, Black Baron&quot;, is a Soviet march with words by Pavel Gorinshtein and music by Samuel Pokrass. It was written in 1920, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>, as a combat anthem for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. Its immediate occasion was the last offensive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-wrangel">Pyotr Wrangel</a>'s White troops from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in July 1920; &quot;Black Baron&quot; was Wrangel's nickname, from his reputed liking for black uniforms, and a verse echoes the Revolutionary Military Council's call for a final effort in the Crimea, published in Pravda on 10 July. The offensive was halted, and Wrangel's army, pursued by the Red Army and by the cavalry and infantry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>'s insurgents, fell back into the Crimea in November 1920 and was evacuated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 14 November.</p><p>The tune may draw on Eastern European Jewish folk music: one passage is also heard in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> song &quot;Dus Zekele mit Koilen&quot; (The Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> Sack). The song became popular in the first years of the Soviet state. It was sung in 1923 at a rally in Leningrad against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>'s ultimatum to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which gave one of its lines new weight, and Nadezhda Krupskaya, Lenin's wife, named it in a letter to a school for blind pupils in the Vologda region as one of her favourite songs, beside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-internationale">The Internationale</a>&quot;. One of its phrases passed into common use and was taken up by other writers, among them V. A. Lugovsky in his 1926 poem &quot;Pesni o vetre&quot;.</p><p>Passed on by ear from 1920 to 1925, the song changed along the way; Gorinshtein later recalled that his original had four or five verses and a slightly different refrain. It was first printed in 1925 and then under several titles, among them &quot;Krasnaya armiya&quot; and &quot;Krasnoarmeyskaya&quot;; only in 1937 did &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest</a>&quot; become its settled title. From the 1920s to the 1940s it was printed without its authors' names, until in the 1950s the musicologist A. Shilov established that they were Gorinshtein and Pokrass.</p><p>The march travelled with the communist movement. German communists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> sang it in a free German translation in the 1920s. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, after the July Revolt of 1927, Fritz Brügel wrote new words to the tune, &quot;Die Arbeiter von Wien&quot; (The Workers of Vienna), which became popular among Austrian socialists and with the Republikanischer Schutzbund, who fought the Dollfuss regime in the brief Austrian Civil War of February 1934; that version was later adapted into Turkish as &quot;Avusturya İşçi Marşı&quot;. The Chapaev Battalion of the International <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigades</a> adopted the march in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, and the Czech communist Julius Fučík is said to have sung it in a Nazi torture chamber. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> new Russian words were set to the tune, among them Pyotr Belyi's of 1941. The Corsican group Chjami Aghjalesi later set its patriotic song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgu">Borgu</a>&quot;, which commemorates the Battle of Borgo, to the same melody.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A combat march of the Civil War presenting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> as stronger than all its enemies; brisk, defiant and martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="1978551" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="12397099" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/The-red-army-choir-the-red-army-is-the-strongest-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-the-red-army-choir-the-red-army-is-the-strongest-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="zlOoMulTsrU" data-title="The Red Army Is the Strongest: a choir recording"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">a choir recording</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Release - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zlOoMulTsrU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Weißes Gesindel und adlige Brut (German communists, Weimar Republic)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1920s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">free translation, same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pesni o vetre (V. A. Lugovsky, poem)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1926</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a phrase of the song quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die Arbeiter von Wien (Fritz Brügel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1927</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new German words, same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Avusturya İşçi Marşı</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Turkish adaptation of Die Arbeiter von Wien</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Vsem nam svoboda i chest' doroga (Pyotr Belyi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Russian words, same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Borgu (Chjami Aghjalesi)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dus Zekele mit Koilen (Yiddish song)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">shares a passage of the melody (possible source)</span></li></ul></section>
